#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Send pages, files and text between a phone and the FlickOS computer.

The phone runs the KDE Connect app (F-Droid, Google Play), unchanged; this
program speaks the part of the KDE Connect protocol (version 8) that moving
things needs: pairing, kdeconnect.share.request (a page, text, a file),
kdeconnect.clipboard and kdeconnect.ping, plus the phone's battery (for the
panel button's tooltip), kdeconnect.findmyphone.request (ring the phone),
kdeconnect.sftp (browse the phone's files) and what the phone plays
(kdeconnect.mpris in: its title, play, pause, next). The phone's
notifications (kdeconnect.notification), calls (kdeconnect.telephony) and
the phone's control of what plays here (kdeconnect.mpris out) are announced
only while that setting is on. Named commands (kdeconnect.runcommand) are
announced only while the user has added one: the phone is sent the names
and asks by key, and the command line stays here. Nothing else is announced, so the phone's app hides its other features
(remote input, replies, ...).

The panel button (only while on) opens the phone popup. `flickos-phone menu`
stays the fallback when that popup isn't there, and the command line: send
files, typed text, the clipboard or a screenshot, ring the phone, browse its
files, what came from it, Settings,
and Show Phone Screen while a program called flickos-phone-screen is installed
(SCREEN_COMMAND; the phone screen add-on, which this package doesn't name anywhere else),
with Use as Second Screen, or Stop Second Screen while SECOND_SCREEN_ON exists.
The same actions are ACTIONS: `actions --json` prints them, and the menu,
the popup and the Phone window draw that list. The Phone window (`window`, code in
usr/share/flickos/phone/window.py, loaded only by that command) shows them
as tiles, plus everything received. *Phone* in the apps menu is that window,
and only while this daemon runs (the desktop file's TryExec is the
flickos-phone-on link). One window per session.

Off by default (flickos-phone set enabled yes). While off nothing runs. While
on, `flickos-phone daemon` (started by FlickOS's autostart, never in the live
session) runs for the session: Python's standard library, no GTK. Only
`send --choose` and the Phone window open GTK, each in its own process.
It listens
only while the computer is on a network where a phone was paired (the
NetworkManager connections in `networks`), or while `flickos-phone pair` runs.

How a phone connects (docs/design/flickos-phone.md, ADR-001 and ADR-003):
the daemon broadcasts its identity to UDP 1716 and the phone connects to TCP
1716. In KDE Connect the device that opens the TCP connection is the TLS
*server*, so the daemon is always the TLS client: it never connects to a
phone's main port, and answers a phone's broadcast with its own identity sent
straight back, so the phone connects. That is what lets Python's ssl module
check an unknown phone's certificate (for pairing) without a verify callback.
A paired phone's certificate is pinned (compared byte for byte).

Files: identity and paired phones in $XDG_DATA_HOME/flickos/phone/, what came
from a phone in $XDG_STATE_HOME/flickos/phone/received.jsonl, settings in
/etc/xdg/flickos/phone.conf then ~/.config/flickos/phone ([Phone]), the
daemon's lock and control socket in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flickos/phone/. Files a
phone sends go into the Downloads folder. Text a click copies, except a text
that is one address of a scheme another package registered in
/usr/share/flickos/phone/open-schemes/SCHEME (a file holding the app's name):
a click opens that with xdg-open. Every change of a setting is recorded for
Settings > History.

Commands:
  daemon             the session daemon (autostart; exits at once while off)
  get KEY            print a setting: enabled, name, networks, notifications,
                     notification-apps, calls, media, commands
  set KEY VALUE      change one ("default" for the default; networks 'UUID ...',
                     notification-apps one name per line, commands JSON)
  notifications list|allow NAME|block NAME
                     which of the phone's apps may show notifications here
  commands list|add NAME COMMAND|remove KEY
                     the named commands the phone may run
  status [--json|--panel]
                     on or off, listening, connected phones; --panel for waybar
  menu               the panel button's menu (fuzzel; the popup's fallback)
  actions [--json] [--to ID]
                     the actions the window, the popup and the menu share
  mute [--to ID]     silence a ringing phone's ringer
  player play|pause|play-pause|next|previous|volume PERCENT [--to ID]
                     what the phone plays (the phone popup's row)
  window [--page overview|received]
                     the Phone window (one per session; a second raises it)
  state              everything the Settings page shows, as one JSON object
  pair [--json]      let a phone pair: wait for it to ask, show its key
  devices [--json]   the paired phones
  forget ID          forget a paired phone
  network list|add [UUID]|remove UUID
                     the networks where it listens
  send [FILE|URL ...] [--text T|--clipboard|--screenshot|--choose|--type-text]
                     send to the phone (--to ID, --notify: say what happened)
  ring [--to ID]     ring the phone, even when it's silenced
  browse [--to ID]   open the phone's files in the file manager
  cancel ID          stop a file on its way to or from a phone (ids in state)
  ping [TEXT]        show TEXT as a notification on the phone
  received [--json]  what came from a phone recently

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 error, 2 usage error.
"""

import argparse
import base64
import configparser
import contextlib
import dataclasses
import datetime
